<GuideLayout slug="reduce-input-lag-pc-gaming">
  <p>
    Input lag isn't one setting — it's a chain from the moment you move your mouse to the
    moment a new photon hits your eye. Every link adds milliseconds. Most "reduce input lag"
    guides throw 20 tweaks at you with no sense of which ones matter. Here they are, ranked by
    how much they actually move the needle.
  </p>

  <h2>The big three (do these first)</h2>
  <h3>1. Turn on the GPU low-latency / Reflex mode</h3>
  <p>
    If your game has NVIDIA Reflex (or the AMD/Intel equivalent), enable it. It caps the render
    queue so the GPU isn't working frames ahead of your inputs. This is the single biggest,
    easiest latency win available — bigger than any registry tweak.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Kill V-Sync, cap your FPS just under your refresh</h3>
  <p>
    V-Sync adds a full frame (or more) of latency. Turn it off. To avoid tearing without it,
    cap your framerate a few FPS below your monitor's refresh rate (e.g. 141 on a 144 Hz panel)
    so the GPU never fully saturates. This combo — Reflex on, V-Sync off, capped FPS — is the
    competitive standard.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Actually run at your monitor's real refresh rate</h3>
  <p>
    Sounds obvious, but Windows silently defaults plenty of 144/240 Hz monitors back to 60 Hz.
    Check Display Settings → Advanced. A 60→240 Hz fix alone removes more perceived lag than
    every micro-tweak combined.
  </p>

  <h2>The small stuff (real, but minor)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mouse polling rate</strong> — 1000 Hz is plenty; chasing 4000/8000 Hz can cost more CPU than it saves in latency.</li>
    <li><strong>Frame pacing / stutter</strong> — consistent frametimes feel lower-latency than a high-but-spiky average. Smoothing 1% lows matters more than chasing peak FPS.</li>
    <li><strong>Background load</strong> — a pegged CPU delays input processing. Close the genuine hogs (not "every background app").</li>
    <li><strong>Wired peripherals + a good USB port</strong> — wireless is fine now, but a flaky port or hub adds jitter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What doesn't help (skip it)</h2>
  <p>
    Random "input lag" registry packs, mouse "acceleration fix" .reg files of unknown origin,
    and disabling services wholesale — these mostly do nothing measurable and occasionally
    break things. If a fix can't tell you <em>which</em> millisecond it removes, be skeptical.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Reflex on, V-Sync off, FPS capped under your real refresh rate. Get those three right and
    you've fixed 90% of input lag before touching anything advanced.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    The advanced 10% — DPC latency, frame pacing, power-plan behavior — is measurable but
    fiddly, which is what <strong>Optimizationmaxxing</strong> is for: it probes your actual
    latency, applies only documented changes, and snapshots every one so you can roll back if a
    tweak doesn't help on your rig.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
